      "title": "Hypothetical VRAM Planning Checklist for Local LLMs (12GB GPU)",
      "body": "This post outlines a hypothetical VRAM planning checklist for running local LLMs, specifically considering a scenario with a 12 GB GPU. It's crucial to remember that no actual models or runtimes were tested; this is a theoretical framework.\n\nThe primary goal is to allocate VRAM effectively. We propose separating allocations into three key areas: model weights, the KV cache, and other runtime overheads.\n\nModel Weights: This is the largest component, directly dependent on the model's parameter count and quantization. For a 12GB card, larger quantized models might fit, but require careful consideration.\n\nKV Cache: This scales with context length and batch size. Longer contexts or larger batches will consume significantly more VRAM, potentially pushing out larger models.\n\nOther Runtime Allocations: This includes gradients (if training/fine-tuning), optimizer states, and framework overhead. These are often overlooked but can be substantial.\n\nThis hypothetical checklist aims to provide a structured approach to VRAM management. What factors do others consider when planning VRAM usage for local LLMs?",
      "fact_refs": [
